#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119548 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c8e217c-252d-529f-a810-84787e2ee886" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Vector is in the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "244793f6-41c3-5920-9264-bec32cda9c9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4807" :question/problem "The matrix $B$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below.\nDetermine if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(B)$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$, where $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}=x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2$, and $\\mathbf{b}_1$, $\\mathbf{b}_2$ are the respective columns of $B$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 15 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(B)$, we need to check if the equation\n\n$$\nB\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}B \\, & \\, \\mathbf{b}\\end{bmatrix}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}B & b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 15 & -6 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}↔R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{5x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 7; x_{2} = 0; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the true statement $0=0$. So the system is equivalent to the following:\n\n$$\n{5x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 7; x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=0$. Substituting into the first equation gives\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 7 5\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation\n\n$$\nB\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Col}(B)$.\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119549 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87dc8398-fb5c-564b-a796-db15ff30d72c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Vector Is in the Column Space of a Larger Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "12f222d8-5563-544a-8154-fb1f9406b231" :question/math-academy-id "e-4808" :question/problem "The matrix $C$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(C)$. In affirmative case, find the value of $x_1 \\cdot x_2 \\cdot x_3$, where $x_1$, $x_2$, and $x_3$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}=x_1\\mathbf{c}_1+x_2\\mathbf{c}_3+x_3\\mathbf{c}_3$, and $\\mathbf{c}_1$, $\\mathbf{c}_2$, and $\\mathbf{c}_3$ are the respective columns of $C$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(C)$, we need to check if the equation\n\n$$\nC\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}C \\, & \\, \\mathbf{b}\\end{bmatrix}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}C & b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + x_{2} + 4x_{3} = 5; 3x_{2} + 4x_{3} = 1; 5x_{3} = 5\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3=1$. Substituting into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x_{2} + 4(1) &= 1 \\\\\n3x_{2} &= -3 \\\\\nx_{2} &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= 5 - x_{2} - 4x_{3} \\\\\n&= 5 - (-1) - 4(1) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation\n\n$$\nC\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Col}(C)$.\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{c}_1$, $\\mathbf{c}_2$, and $\\mathbf{c}_3$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 \\cdot x_3 = 2 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot 1 = -2\n$$\n\n."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a557ee8d-51a9-5387-a28c-67ffefed55b4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given a Vector That’s in the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "b6d52e39-a8a0-5048-80a1-a342a2617e0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6208" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 + k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, then the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nmust be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}A \\, & \\, \\mathbf{b}\\end{bmatrix}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}A & b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 + k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -32 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 + k\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 5R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -32 \\\\ 0 & -11 & k - 48\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=\\frac{1}{8}R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -11 & k - 48\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 11R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k - 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 2x_{2} = 10; x_{2} = 4; 0 = k - 4\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-4$, which means that $k=4$."}]}
